json_serialize(obj, flags = 0)

Serialize a Pyton object into a JSON string. By default, unsupported types are
serialized as `null` and dict keys which aren't strings are skipped.

Parameters
----------
obj
	A Python object.
flags : int, optional
	A set of integer flags which can be used to select an ouput form and to
        modify the handling of unsupported types and non-string dictionary keys.

	The following output formats are supported:

	UJ_FMT_FAST (0)
        	Serialize dict key-value pairs in the order they're returned by
                `PyDict_Next` and don't include any redundant whitespace in the
                output.

	UJ_FMT_DET (1)
        	Serialize dict key-value pairs in deterministic order. Dict keys
                will be sorted based on Unicode codepoint comparisons.

	UJ_FMT_PRETTY (2)
        	Like UJ_FMT_DET but include redundant whiteapce in the generated
                string to make it easier to read for humans. To avoid creating
                extremely huge strings mostly composed of space characters for
                large and complex input objects, indentation is done with sequences
                of TAB (ASCII 09) characters.
                
                
	